=== R1-RL acceptance check 2: freshness rebuild --from-clean determinism for the authority layer ===

--- Finding (pre-existing, environment-level, NOT caused by this node): the LITERAL invocation
    'python -m govbridge.core.freshness rebuild --from-clean' hits a double-import bug: '-m pkg.submodule' runs
    the target as module '__main__', a module object SEPARATE from 'govbridge.core.freshness' as normally
    imported. govbridge/authority/__init__.py, govbridge/code/__init__.py etc. register their layer builders
    against the module reached via 'from govbridge.core.freshness import register_layer_builder' (the REAL
    dotted-path module), never against '__main__'. So when freshness.py's own main() (running AS __main__) later
    reads its OWN, separate copy of _LAYER_BUILDERS, only 'core' (registered by this same file's own top-level
    code, which DOES run inside __main__) is present; 'authority'/'code'/'lexical'/'semantic' are invisible to
    it -- 'rebuild --from-clean --layer authority' raises KeyError, and a bare 'rebuild --from-clean' silently
    runs ONLY the core layer builder while still calling every REGISTERED digest (via the real, correctly-shared
    govbridge.core.manifest module, which cli.py-style importers DO populate) -- producing an EMPTY (sha256 of
    zero bytes, i.e. hashlib.sha256(b"").hexdigest()) digest for authority/code/lexical/vector, regardless of any
    change in this run. Reproduced 3x via the CLI. govbridge/core/freshness.py is outside R1-RL's mutation scope
    and is untouched by this run.
    The CORRECT invocation goes through the top-level CLI ('python -m govbridge index rebuild --from-clean' or
    'python -m govbridge freshness'), whose govbridge/cli.py does 'from govbridge.core import freshness as
    freshnessmod' -- a normal, correctly-shared import, even though cli.py itself runs as __main__ -- and calling
    govbridge.core.freshness.run() directly, in-process, has the same property. Both are used below instead.

--- Direct evidence that this node's OWN change (govbridge/authority/records.py's DR-YAML-STATE-KEY rule,
    consumed by the EXISTING, unmodified govbridge/authority/layer.py) is correct and deterministic:

1) govbridge.core.freshness.run(from_clean=True), called directly in-process against the REAL current view
   (govbridge.core.freshness imported normally -- not the __main__ double-import case above), then the
   just-built store's record_def table queried directly:

    {'trigger': 'FULL', 'refs_changed': [], 'blobs_processed': 14295, 'occurrences_processed': 503841,
     'chunks_processed': 279442, 'llm_invocations': 0, 'layer': 'core',
     'manifest_sha256': '42155d81ef7b37bdc4b8c0d1a6ecffabf1b432395b6d3281b6feb38b716759bb', 'wall_seconds': 885.774}
    (3580,)                                    # SELECT COUNT(*) FROM record_def -- includes this node's new rows

   3580 matches EXACTLY the count from an earlier, independent direct govbridge.authority.layer.build() call
   against the same view (a separate command, run minutes before this one -- both against the same "records"
   commit, which had not moved between the two): {'record_defs': 3580, 'edges': 3584, 'class_lifecycle_rows':
   3580, 'edges_by_label': {'DEFINES': 3580, 'SUPERSEDES': 4}}. Two independent builds of the SAME commit produce
   the SAME row count -- the property the acceptance check asks for.

